Step 1 — Inventory the agent

Look at what's actually running and where it's installed. These are passive, read-only and won't trigger anything:

Get-Process | Where-Object { $_.ProcessName -match 'Sentinel|SentinelAgent|SentinelServiceHost|StaticEngine|Agent' } | Select-Object ProcessName, Id, Path
Get-Service | Where-Object { $_.DisplayName -match 'Sentinel' } | Select-Object Status, Name, DisplayName
Get-ChildItem 'C:\Program Files\SentinelOne' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Select-Object Name

Note specifically:
- The agent version (look in the install dir or Get-Item the exe's version info) — newer versions have killed off older evasion families, so this tells us what's viable.
- Whether there's a tamper-protection / self-protect service and whether it's enforcing.

Also check your own effective posture (still passive):

whoami /groups | findstr /i "S-1-5-32-544"   # are you admin?
Get-ItemProperty 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\PowerShell' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
$ExecutionContext.SessionState.LanguageMode   # ConstrainedLanguage vs FullLanguage

